module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_host_joined_off_the_route_carries_no_tag_the_acl_grants_on

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode, BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, StructurallyGuaranteed }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef }

data a_host_joined_off_the_route_carries_no_tag_the_acl_grants_on: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  // THE JOIN WAS DONE BY HAND, THE ACL GRANTS BY TAG, AND EVERY LATER JUDGMENT ABOUT THE HOST
  // INHERITED THE GAP. The host was a member; the fleet's grants do not speak about members.

  identity: "a_host_joined_off_the_route_carries_no_tag_the_acl_grants_on" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "fleet membership is granted only through gunbc.tailscale_join_converge, which joins with a key carrying the fleet node tag and reports a node that joined without it as a refusal rather than a success; and the reach judgment that would otherwise read 'answers ICMP, refuses TCP' as a network fault names it instead as an ACL standing, so the gap is said at the moment a roster would rely on the host"
    }
  },

  receipts: [
    "**INVALID STATE: a host is on the tailnet -- it answers ping, it resolves, it is listed -- and it is refused every granted connection, because the grants are written against a TAG and the node carries no tag. The membership fact and the grant fact are both true and neither mentions the other, so the operator sees a working network and a dead host.**",

    "FOUND: 2026-11-21, butch (operator_host_srv14), during a lane's bring-up. An interactive `tailscale up` had enrolled the node; the fleet's ACLs (extdeps.tailscale.acl, extdeps.tailscale.acl_api) grant by tag, so an untagged node sits outside every grant. The symptom that reached the lane was a TCP refusal under a successful ping, which reads as a network fault and invites network debugging that cannot succeed.",

    "THE RECOGNITION RULE THE REPORT STATES, WORTH KEEPING VERBATIM IN THE ROW: pings but refuses TCP = ACL, not network. ICMP reaching a host while every granted port refuses is the signature of a grant gap, not of a link gap; the reverse pairing (refuses ICMP, answers a port) points elsewhere.",

    "WHAT THE TREE ALREADY OWNS, SO THIS ROW DOES NOT FORK IT: gunbc.tailscale_join_converge models the fleet node tag, the join states `joined with the required tag` and `joined WITHOUT the required tag`, the expiry of the enrolling key, and a refusal arm for a key/join tag mismatch -- its own commentary states the consequence exactly: a node whose tags do not match the key's leaves 'every reachability judgment this converge then makes about a tag the node is not in'. The model is right; the specimen is a join performed OUTSIDE that route, which is why the model never saw the host. That is the consumption failure, not a modeling gap.",

    "THE OTHER HALF, WHICH IS MINE AND NOT THAT MODULE'S: nothing on the read side refuses an untagged member. A host may sit in the fleet's inventories -- hardware, units, runner slot, memory envelope all present and correct -- while being unreachable by the identity those rows assume. The identity axis of gunbc.plans.desired_state_follows_the_machine is where that is closed: per host, read the node's own tags and the fingerprint it answers with, and refuse admission where they do not match what the model claims.",

    "THE SPECIMEN HAS SINCE MOVED, AND THE ROW MOVES WITH IT (2026-11-21, gunbc.design_ruling::butch_tailnet_join_is_operator_handled_2026_11_21): asked whether butch's join waits on the modelled join route (PR 16820, itself held behind the fleet-converge budget question), the operator said, verbatim, \"I will do the tailnet join + engine retirement now\", and then, verbatim, \"but you'll need to record/model that in the repo\". Butch joins by hand, deliberately, and the repository owes the model the RESULT -- that host's tailnet identity as its fleet anchor -- grounded in reads of the host, never in that row. This sharpens rather than softens the ceiling below: the guarantee was never 'membership arrives only through the route' (a hand-join is legitimate, expected, and now ruled); it is that a hand-joined member is READ, and one carrying no fleet tag is refused by every judgment that relies on the grant. That is the identity axis, not the route.",

    "RUNG, CEILING, AND WHY NOT HIGHER THAN IT IS: found at Mitigatable (re-join the node through the route, or tag it). The ceiling is StructurallyGuaranteed because an untagged fleet member can be made unnameable: if membership is only ever granted by the route that carries the tag, and the reach judgment refuses rather than guesses when a member carries no tag, the state has no route in. It is NOT StructurallyImpossible: an operator can always run `tailscale up` by hand on a host, and this repository does not own the operator's keyboard -- the guarantee is that such a host is refused, not that it cannot happen.",

    "NEXT TRIGGER: the per-host identity read of gunbc.plans.desired_state_follows_the_machine running over both the build and serving rosters and refusing a member outside every grant -- or any further host found reachable-by-ping and refused-by-grant, which is the discovery this row was written from.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.tailscale_join_converge", decl_name: "tailscale_join_converge_mode", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_key_enrollment", decl_name: "enrolled_fingerprint_for", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "butch_tailnet_join_is_operator_handled_2026_11_21", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ]
}
